{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 13, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.6
    ,
      "why": "Best family so far: 9 feasible designs, best power_index 2.45, and 6 of the 9 merged-front points. It emits 1 result/cycle, so throughput >= 50 MSPS is trivially met (Fmax only needs to clear 50 MHz), and the register-every-m-stages knob lets us trade Fmax for FF count and power. The low-power corner is reached with large m and minimal guard bits, so I keep m fully open (2..8) and let the search push it up. data_width/n_iter start at 13/12 because the 2^-12 max-error constraint needs roughly W>=14 with round rounding plus N~W micro-rotations; the upper ends (20/20) exist to populate the higher-accuracy end of the front for hypervolume, not for the selection rule."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 13, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "why": "Second-best family: 20 feasible designs, best power_index 4.04, 3 of 9 merged-front points. It is the fully-registered variant (one stage per micro-rotation), so it costs more FFs/power than pipelined_m at the same accuracy but has the shortest latency and highest Fmax. It still contributes distinct front points, so I keep a meaningful share to avoid collapsing the front onto pipelined_m alone. Same accuracy-driven W/N window (13..20 / 12..20) and full guard/rounding sweep so the two families are compared on identical accuracy settings."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Only pipelined and pipelined_m are allowed for me, and the fleet facts show the iterative/unrolled_k families are entirely infeasible (0 feasible in 100 evals) because a shared datapath cannot reach 50 MSPS. Both of my families are 1-result/cycle, so the throughput constraint is essentially free and the real fight is power_index vs accuracy_bits. pipelined_m already owns the merged front (6/9) and the best power (2.45), so it gets the larger share; pipelined keeps 40% because it still holds 3/9 front points and gives the high-Fmax end of the curve. Accuracy depends only on data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding, so I sweep those identically in both families and let m be the pipelined_m-specific power knob. The window 13..20 for W and 12..20 for N brackets the feasible accuracy threshold (max_abs_err <= 2^-12 needs about W>=14 with round rounding and N~W) while still reaching well past 12 accuracy bits so the hypervolume against the (power 40, accuracy 12) reference is covered across the whole trade-off curve, not just the min-power corner the selection rule will pick."
}

